A||||4||montage||Synchromesh Tranny Fluid Source||||1||
Z||000000||montage||12-26-2000||09:40 AM||||After reading the discussion forum on synchromesh transmission fluid, an alternative source by Pennzoil was suggested. After failing to find a source I'd thought I'd try my local quick lube place that sells Pennzoil products. Low and behold they had a 5 gal bucket of synchromesh transmission fluid and sold me some for $4.00 a quart. <BR>Happy Holidays!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||horseman||12-26-2000||11:44 PM||||Is this the best fluid to use in the muncie 4spd? I sometimes have a sluggish gearbox also. Oh yeah, I have a 84 4spd. with 4.10 gears. Thanks,<BR>Charlie||||reg||5
Z||000002||montage||12-27-2000||08:37 AM||||I have an 88 Formula and my understanding is that GM engineering changed transmission fluid recommendations in 1988 to synchromesh. Since all manuals have similar construction I would assume this recommendation would also apply to all other manual transmissions that recommended 5W30 or ATF. The big argument that I see is the oil being more compatible with the non-ferrous metals improving shifting and possibly reducing wear.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Will||12-27-2000||09:50 AM||||I've changed both of my Getrags from whatever was in before to ATF and they became much easier, smoother, and faster to shift. Does synchromesh fluid have similar results?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||2birds||12-27-2000||10:41 PM||||My Getrag has always had plain old 5W-30 and nothing else, and I used some Havoline full synthetic the last time around since I had some left over. No difference I could tell.  My only complaint is that it has always seemed kind of notchy.  I've always changed it roughly once a year/15,000 miles.  Any suggestions?||||reg||1
